<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-top: -15px; margin-bottom: 30px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Scenario:</b> Sustainable Fashion</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Theme:</b> How History Is Fashionable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; " colspan="3">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Inform others about environmental or community issues. Interactive: Engage in discussions on global topics like environmental awareness.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can inform peers about the impact of certain fashion practices (e.g., "Vintage clothes reduce waste because they are reused.")
                    Can engage in a discussion to explain how sustainable fashion benefits the environment (e.g., "Recycled fabrics will help conserve water and energy in the production process.")
                    Can discuss sustainable fashion trends and give opinions (e.g., "I believe more brands will prioritize sustainable materials in their designs to reduce environmental impact.").</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images featuring fashion from different historical periods (e.g., 70s bell-bottoms, 90s grunge) alongside modern sustainable fashion items (e.g., vintage denim, recycled fabric dresses). Students are asked to briefly discuss in pairs what they "notice" about the styles and how they might "reflect" personality or history. The teacher prompts with questions like, "What were people wearing in the 70s? What is trendy today?"</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences comparing past and present fashion trends, incorporating target grammar and vocabulary. "In the early 2000s, people were wearing low-rise jeans, but today, both flared and ripped jeans are in style. Before eco-friendly fashion gained popularity, brands had primarily focused on aesthetics over sustainability." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /v/ in "vintage" and /w/ in "wear", and demonstrates stress in "fashionable" and "sustainability."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and grammar.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'vintage' fashion old or new?" (Expected answer: "Old but still stylish.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a brand 'launches' a new collection, does it mean they are ending it or introducing it?" (Expected answer: "Introducing it.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'people were wearing low-rise jeans,' am I talking about the past or the present?" (Expected answer: "The past, an ongoing action.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Sustainable Fashion".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio clip (or transcript) of a dialogue between two teenagers discussing a local "Vintage & Sustainable Fashion Show" they attended. The dialogue naturally incorporates vocabulary such as "vintage," "retro," "eco-friendly," "recycled materials," "sustainability," "evolve," "predict," and grammatical structures like past continuous, past perfect, and future simple when describing the show and future trends.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Productive and Interactive Speaking. Students will practice informing and engaging in discussions related to environmental awareness through fashion.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main topic of Maria and Carlos's conversation?" (Expected answer: "A vintage and sustainable fashion show.") "What did they think about the show?" (Expected answer: "They thought it was innovative and inspiring.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific questions:</b> "According to Maria, what had fashion brands focused on before the sustainability movement gained traction?" (Expected answer: "Primarily on aesthetics.") "What prediction does Carlos make about future fashion trends?" (Expected answer: "He believes more brands will prioritize sustainable materials.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking: Practice informing and discussing with accurate use of past continuous with present simple to compare trends, past perfect for completed actions, and future simple for predictions. Focus on vocabulary (fashion, trend, vintage, retro, sustainability, materials, eco-friendly, evolve, recycle, wear, reflect, adopt). Pronunciation practice of /v/ in "vintage" and "evolve" versus /w/ in "wear" and "waste," and stress in "fashionable," "sustainability," "eco-friendly."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Builders):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence starters or cues, and students complete the sentences orally, focusing on the target grammar. For example: "In the 90s, people were wearing... (flannel shirts). Today, they prefer..." "Before sustainability became a major trend, many brands had not... (considered eco-friendly materials)." "In the future, I believe fashion will... (continue to evolve)." Teacher circulates, providing immediate feedback on grammar and pronunciation.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled (Compare & Predict Pictures):</b> The teacher displays pairs of images showing historical fashion styles and their modern sustainable equivalents (e.g., a picture of a 60s mini-skirt and a modern mini-skirt made from recycled fabric). In pairs, students describe what people *were wearing* in the past, what they *are wearing* now, how these styles *reflect* "history," and *predict* how they *will evolve* to become more "sustainable" in the "future." They are encouraged to use specific vocabulary like "vintage," "retro," "eco-friendly," and "recycled." Emphasis is placed on pronouncing /v/ and /w/ distinctively and stressing multisyllabic words.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Mini-Discussion Prompts):</b> Students engage in short, structured discussions with a partner using prompts like: "How have fashion trends evolved from the past to today, specifically in terms of sustainability?" "Discuss how using eco-friendly fabrics can benefit the environment." "What future trend do you predict will gain the most popularity in sustainable fashion?" The teacher encourages them to use the pragmatic functions of asking about and discussing fashion trends and eco-friendly choices.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Sustainable Fashion".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Fashion Forward Discussion Panel." Students work in groups of 3-4 to prepare for a mini-discussion panel. Each group will represent a specific aspect of sustainable fashion from a historical perspective (e.g., Group 1: "The Rise of Vintage & Retro," Group 2: "Innovation in Recycled Materials," Group 3: "The Future of Eco-friendly Brands").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                        <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups spend 5-7 minutes discussing and organizing their main points and arguments based on their assigned topic, integrating target grammar and vocabulary. They should prepare to discuss "how fashion had evolved," "what people were wearing," and "what changes they predict for the future."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then presents their ideas for 2-3 minutes to the class or another group, acting as a panel. Following their presentation, they engage in a brief Q&A or discussion with the "audience" (other students).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use sociolinguistic aspects by acknowledging different values: "Some people say fast fashion is cheap and convenient, while others describe it as harmful and wasteful, showing how language reflects different values about sustainability." They should also use phrases for giving opinions and making predictions (e.g., "I believe...", "In my opinion...", "Fashion trends will continue to...").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing fluency, collaborative interaction, and general communicative effectiveness, intervening only to facilitate or encourage, not to correct errors immediately.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe each student's participation in the "Fashion Forward Discussion Panel," noting their use of target language and communicative effectiveness.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' participation and contribution to the "Fashion Forward Discussion Panel," where they inform peers about fashion practices and engage in discussions on global topics like environmental awareness.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, particularly of contrastive sounds (/v/ and /w/) and stress in multisyllabic words.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and varied use of target vocabulary (e.g., sustainable, vintage, retro, eco-friendly, evolve, predict).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of grammar: past continuous with present simple to compare trends, past perfect for completed actions, and future simple for predictions.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in expressing opinions, providing information, and making predictions.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and collaborative interaction within the group, demonstrating engagement in discussion.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vocabulary related to sustainable fashion did I use effectively today in my discussions?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confidently did I compare past and present fashion trends, and predict future ones, using the correct grammar (e.g., 'were wearing,' 'had adopted,' 'will evolve')?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about discussing sustainable fashion and historical trends today, and how did I overcome it?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has my understanding of the environmental impact of fashion and the value of sustainable practices evolved after today's discussion?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to spontaneously use the target grammar (past continuous vs. present simple, past perfect, future simple) during discussions, particularly when comparing and predicting trends?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did they engage effectively in discussions about the environmental benefits of sustainable fashion, showing clear understanding and expressing opinions? What specific areas need more scaffolding?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments can I make to further encourage fluency and deeper interaction in future speaking activities?</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), we will use the ideas, vocabulary, and grammatical structures we discussed today to write a short essay or a blog post about the evolution of sustainable fashion, focusing on expressing opinions and making informed predictions about its future."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>